---
import { Picture } from "astro:assets";
import { render, type CollectionEntry } from "astro:content";
import PhotoSlot from "./PhotoSlot.astro";

/**
 * One plant in the offer: photograph on the left, everything we know about it on the right.
 *
 * This is the pattern carried over from the current site and the reason people find it in
 * search - a concrete cultivation description under the plant's name. The text is the owners'
 * own, word for word, but it is no longer the long copy the old site ran: they asked in
 * September 2026 for the entries to be made compact and uniform, so thirty-two of the
 * thirty-nine were cut to two paragraphs and 66-78 words and the remaining seven, already
 * shorter than that, were left alone. **The cut removed whole sentences and rewrote none** -
 * every surviving word is theirs - and what came out is listed in docs/inwentaryzacja.md under
 * "Skrócenie opisów" for them to review. Cutting further is theirs to ask for, not ours to do,
 * and CLAUDE.md says what a further cut would cost.
 *
 * Two things changed with the move to real category pages:
 *
 * - the heading level is a prop. On a category page the plant sits directly under the
 *   page's `h1`, so it is an `h2`; the level is passed in rather than hardcoded, because a
 *   heading that is right in one place and wrong in another is the defect this had before.
 * - the group chip is off by default. On `/kwiaty-balkonowe/` every entry is balkonowe;
 *   the chip repeated the page's own title once per plant. It stays available for a mixed
 *   listing (search results, a future "wszystkie rośliny" page).
 *
 * The photograph is the entry's own when it has one. `image` and `imageAlt` come from the
 * frontmatter and the schema keeps them together, so the branch is on the pair, not on one
 * of them; an entry without a photograph falls back to the striped `PhotoSlot`, which is now
 * a waiting state rather than the default. **Nothing on the site takes that branch today** -
 * the last eighteen gaps were filled with stand-ins in September 2026 - but it stays, because
 * the next plant the owners add will arrive before its picture does, exactly as every other
 * one has.
 *
 * **`imageCredit` is why there is a `<figcaption>` here again, and it is not the caption that
 * was removed.** The owners had the one-sentence editorial captions taken off every entry in
 * September 2026; this line is attribution for a file that is not theirs, which CC BY and
 * CC BY-SA require in exchange for the right to publish it at all. It is printed only where
 * the frontmatter carries a credit, so the twenty-one photographs from the holding still show
 * a bare picture, and it leaves with the borrowed file rather than being edited away. The same
 * shape of argument `History.astro` makes for its own surviving caption - and the same warning:
 * this is not licence to put editorial captions back.
 *
 * The file name is the anchor, so /kwiaty-balkonowe/#fuksja links straight at one plant.
 */
interface Props {
  entry: CollectionEntry<"plants">;
  /** Heading level for the plant's name. 2 under a page `h1`. */
  level?: 2 | 3 | 4;
  /** Show the group chip - only where the listing mixes groups. */
  showGroup?: boolean;
  /** The first entry on a category page: its photograph is the likely LCP element, so it
   *  is fetched eagerly instead of being pushed behind the lazy queue. */
  priority?: boolean;
  /** Show a "wróć do spisu" link back to the category page's `#offer-title` - only where
   *  `OfferSection` also rendered its spis (more than one entry in the group), otherwise
   *  there is nothing at the top worth returning to. */
  backToIndex?: boolean;
}

const { entry, level = 2, showGroup = false, priority = false, backToIndex = false } = Astro.props;
const { name, group, slot, image, imageAlt, imageCredit, facts } = entry.data;
const { Content } = await render(entry);

/**
 * **One ratio for every photograph in the offer: 4:3.**
 *
 * This is the handoff's own figure (docs/design/README.md) and it is back after a detour. The
 * frame used to follow each file - its own ratio, floored at 3:4 - and the owners chose that in
 * September 2026 precisely because a flat 4:3 crops most of what they supplied. They reversed it
 * later the same month, having seen both, and asked for every entry photograph to be the same
 * size. Uniformity won over fidelity to each frame; that is their call to make and it is written
 * down in docs/inwentaryzacja.md with the measurements.
 *
 * **What it costs, measured rather than remembered.** Of the twenty-one photographs in the
 * collection today, exactly one is already 4:3 (werbena) and survives untouched. Eighteen are
 * taller than 4:3 and keep 52-61% of their height; the two landscape files are cropped on width
 * instead - chryzantema wielkokwiatowa keeps 89%, pelargonia rabatowa 75%. The September 2026
 * batch of nine changed the arithmetic but not the shape of it: those sources are mostly 3:4
 * phone frames, so they land at 55-60%, and the one near-landscape frame among them
 * (petunia-surfinia, 1.21) keeps 91%.
 *
 * **The crop is centred and cannot be aimed.** There is no `object-position` here and adding one
 * would not rescue the tall frames: aiming moves the band, and no position helps when the bloom
 * is simply taller than the window. If a particular entry turns out to be clipped somewhere
 * ruinous, the fix is a re-crop of the source file, not a per-entry exception - the whole point
 * of this rule is that there are no per-entry exceptions.
 *
 * **What it buys**, and it is the reason they asked: `PhotoSlot` has always drawn its pending
 * frame at 4:3, so a page holding both photographs and placeholders used to alternate between
 * two box shapes down the column. Now it does not. With 18 of the 39 entries still waiting for a
 * picture, that column is more placeholder than photograph, and it is now one straight edge.
 */
const PHOTO_RATIO = 4 / 3;

/** `width`/`height` describe the **box**, not the file - otherwise the browser reserves the
 *  file's height and the text under the photograph jumps when it loads. */
const PHOTO_WIDTH = 560;

const Heading = `h${level}` as "h2" | "h3" | "h4";
---

<article class="entry" id={entry.id}>
  <figure class="entry__figure">
    {
      image && imageAlt ? (
        <Picture
          class="entry__photo"
          src={image}
          formats={["avif", "webp"]}
          fallbackFormat="jpg"
          widths={[560, 1120]}
          sizes="(min-width: 1200px) 578px, (min-width: 700px) 45vw, 90vw"
          width={PHOTO_WIDTH}
          height={Math.round(PHOTO_WIDTH / PHOTO_RATIO)}
          alt={imageAlt}
          loading={priority ? "eager" : "lazy"}
          fetchpriority={priority ? "high" : undefined}
        />
      ) : (
        <PhotoSlot ratio="4 / 3" label={slot} stripe={10} boxed />
      )
    }

    {
      image && imageCredit && (
        <figcaption class="entry__credit">
          fot. <a href={imageCredit.sourceUrl}>{imageCredit.author}</a>, Wikimedia Commons ·{" "}
          {imageCredit.licenseUrl ? (
            <a href={imageCredit.licenseUrl}>{imageCredit.license}</a>
          ) : (
            imageCredit.license
          )}{" "}
          · kadr
        </figcaption>
      )
    }
  </figure>

  <div class="entry__text">
    <div class="entry__head">
      <Heading class="entry__name">{name}</Heading>
      {showGroup && <span class="entry__group">{group}</span>}
    </div>

    <div class="entry__body copy">
      <Content />
    </div>

    {
      facts && (
        <dl class="facts">
          {facts.map((fact) => (
            <div class="facts__item">
              <dt>{fact.label}</dt>
              <dd>{fact.value}</dd>
            </div>
          ))}
        </dl>
      )
    }

    {
      backToIndex && (
        <p class="entry__back">
          <a class="cta-line" href="#offer-title">
            Wróć do spisu roślin
          </a>
        </p>
      )
    }
  </div>
</article>

<style>
  /* **The only rule inside an entry, and that is what makes it readable.** The handoff draws
     this separator and the one above `.facts` identically (1px `#E0DDCE`). In two columns that
     works, because the facts rule spans the text column while this one spans the page. In one
     column they collapse onto the same width and become the same mark, and the reader cannot
     tell "the facts follow" from "a different plant starts here" - which the owners reported.
     There is no breakpoint to fix it in, so one of the two had to go, and the one that carries
     less meaning went: see `.facts`. A line on a category page now says exactly one thing.
     Thickening this one instead was tried and rejected - a 2px rule is a mark this design does
     not otherwise make on a page. */
  .entry {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
    padding-block: clamp(1.75rem, 4vw, 3rem);
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--gap-entry);
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 260px), 1fr));
    /* Without this the photograph stretches to the height of the text beside it. */
    align-items: start;
  }

  .entry__figure {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.6rem;
  }

  /* 4:3, the same on every entry and the same as the `PhotoSlot` that stands in for a missing
     photograph - the note beside `PHOTO_RATIO` above argues why, and what it costs. It is a
     constant rather than a custom property per element because there is no longer anything to
     vary: a ratio written once here and once as width/height on the <Picture> is the whole of
     it, and the two must move together or Astro emits a file the CSS then crops a second time.

     `object-fit: cover` now bites on twenty of the twenty-one photographs; it takes the middle
     band and cannot be aimed. 578px in `sizes` is the photograph column at full width: --edge
     caps the content at 1200px, .entry splits it in two, and --gap-entry takes at most 2.75rem
     out of the middle. */
  .entry__photo {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    aspect-ratio: 4 / 3;
    object-fit: cover;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  /* Attribution, not a caption - the component header says why the distinction matters and
     why this line is allowed to exist. It is set smaller than the one caption on the site
     (History.astro draws its figcaption at 0.8rem) because that one carries something the
     visitor came for and this one is a debt being paid: legible, checkable, and not competing
     with the plant's name. --ink-grey is the token tokens.css reserves for "every uppercase
     label and every photo caption", 4.99:1 on --paper, and every category page is one flat
     light section, so there is no dark ground here to raise it against.

     No rule above it: a category page draws exactly one kind of horizontal line and it means
     "a different plant starts here" (see .entry). The 0.6rem grid gap on .entry__figure is
     what separates this from the photograph. */
  .entry__credit {
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--ink-grey);
    /* The photograph's own width, so a long author name wraps inside the column instead of
       widening the grid track and squeezing the text beside it. */
    max-width: 100%;
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  /* The links inherit the prose green rather than the grey around them - an attribution whose
     two links are invisible is not an attribution. */
  .entry__credit a {
    color: var(--green);
  }

  .entry__text {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.85rem;
    align-content: start;
  }

  /* Baseline, not centre: the serif name and the uppercase chip have very different
     cap heights, and centring them makes the chip look like it is falling. */
  .entry__head {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem 0.9rem;
  }

  /* The size is on the class, not the tag, so the heading level can change without the
     type changing with it. */
  .entry__name {
    font-size: var(--text-h3);
    line-height: 1.1;
  }

  .entry__group {
    font-size: 0.6rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--green);
    background: var(--paper-dim);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule-dim);
    padding: 0.2rem 0.5rem;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .entry__body {
    max-width: 60ch;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  /* **Space, not a line** - a departure from the handoff, which gives this block
     `border-top: 1px solid #E0DDCE` and `padding-top: 0.85rem` (docs/design/README.md). The
     rule is what collided with the entry separator once the columns stacked; the block loses
     nothing by it, because the uppercase `dt` labels already announce that a different kind of
     content starts here. The padding grows to carry the separation the line used to. Chosen by
     the owner in September 2026 from a mock-up of four alternatives; docs/inwentaryzacja.md
     records what the other three were and why this one won. */
  .facts {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 150px), 1fr));
    gap: 0.75rem 1.5rem;
    padding-top: 1.4rem;
  }

  /* `start`, not the default stretch: the item box is stretched to the tallest fact in the
     row, and stretched auto rows push the value away from its label - so a two-line value
     next to a one-line one left the two `dd`s on different lines. */
  .facts__item {
    display: grid;
    align-content: start;
    gap: 0.1rem;
  }

  .facts dt {
    font-size: 0.62rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.16em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-grey);
  }

  .facts dd {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .entry__back {
    margin-top: 0.4rem;
  }
</style>
